            <ArticleTitle>Pseudo-spinal headache.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>358-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Myofascial trigger points are often not considered in the differential diagnosis of headaches.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A patient who presented with severe bifrontal headaches was treated by injections of the sternocleidomastoid muscle trigger points with local anesthetics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The patient experienced complete resolution of all symptoms, which had not reappeared after 14 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Myofascial pain may mimic other disorders, and myofascial headaches can be easily treated once properly diagnosed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
